    <File_Area_Ancillary>
        <File>
            <file_name>albedo_arrokoth4_fp36h2_masked1.fits</file_name>
            <local_identifier>fits_file</local_identifier>
            <creation_date_time>2024-09-30</creation_date_time>
            <file_size unit="byte">2882880</file_size>
            <md5_checksum>a9493604958eb310af790bc182a3b508</md5_checksum>
        </File>
        <Header>
            <offset unit="byte">0</offset>
            <object_length unit="byte">2880</object_length>
            <parsing_standard_id>FITS 4.0</parsing_standard_id>
            <description>
                This albedo map consists of two south polar azimuthal equidistant projections,
                one for each lobe. This projection was used to minimize distortion relative to
                the New Horizons images. A separate projection was used for each lobe to avoid
                any coordinate ambiguity. The UV texture mapping in the OBJ file uses this
                projection. Refer to the pre-print paper Porter, et al (2024b) for more detail.

                Hapke parameters are provided in the header keywords 'PHOTC', 'PHOTB', and 'PHOTH'.
                These keywords correspond to Hapke parameters c, b, and h respectively with values
                c=0.563970266, b=0.355509153, and h=-7.04773385E-06.
            </description>
        </Header>
        <Array_2D>
            <name>32-bit floating point albedo map</name>
            <local_identifier>fits_array</local_identifier>
            <offset unit="byte">2880</offset>
            <axes>2</axes>
            <axis_index_order>Last Index Fastest</axis_index_order>
            <Element_Array>
                <data_type>IEEE754MSBSingle</data_type>
            </Element_Array>
            <Axis_Array>
                <axis_name>Line</axis_name>
                <elements>1200</elements>
                <sequence_number>1</sequence_number>
            </Axis_Array>
            <Axis_Array>
                <axis_name>Sample</axis_name>
                <elements>600</elements>
                <sequence_number>2</sequence_number>
            </Axis_Array>
        </Array_2D>
    </File_Area_Ancillary>
    <File_Area_Ancillary>
        <File>
            <file_name>albedo_arrokoth4_fp36h2_masked1.png</file_name>
            <local_identifier>png_file</local_identifier>
            <creation_date_time>2024-09-30</creation_date_time>
            <file_size unit="byte">200809</file_size>
            <md5_checksum>a59a79eff366f830f2f3fa395ccb7a8d</md5_checksum>
        </File>
        <Encoded_Image>
            <name>Integer-encoded PNG albedo map</name>
            <local_identifier>png_image</local_identifier>
            <offset unit="byte">0</offset>
            <object_length unit="byte">200809</object_length>
            <encoding_standard_id>PNG</encoding_standard_id>
            <description>
                The pixels in this image are encoded as signed 32-bit integers, but only positive
                values are used, meaning the effective range of values is that of 16-bit unsigned
                integers (i.e., 0 to 65535). The actual albedo values (e.g., as presented in the
                data array described above) can be approximately recovered from these integer pixel
                values using the following formula:

                    albedo = (pixel / 1361975.975) + 0.03188
            </description>
        </Encoded_Image>
    </File_Area_Ancillary>
